  "adjustments": [
    {
      "target": "u1",
      "focus": ["D", "S", "C"],
      "change": "Reframe the already-open-container rule so its trigger and follow-up are usable with the Actor's actual information access: read the trigger from the current observation (a container that is already open and was not opened by the immediately preceding action), treat it as already searched (do not examine or re-open it), and instead of stepping to the next container in the same rotation, break the loop by switching to a different kind of location that does not appear in the Actor's recent observations. Keep the missing-object/tool-only scope, and extend the carve-out so it excludes both the examine-task desklamp approach and the pick-and-place receptacle approach.",
      "reason": "The current runs show the loop u1 targets: in `P6` the Actor returns to drawer 1 while it is already open and empty and not just opened (`P6:14`) and repeats the drawer 1-6 rotation (`P6:23`, `P6:38`, `P6:44`); in `P8` it re-visits the already-open drawer 1 (`P8:7`, `P8:11`). The trigger is locally checkable because the current observation states whether the container is open. I localized the follow-up because the Actor receives only the current observation and the last two pre-action observation/action pairs, so u1's original 'not observed earlier in the current search pass' and 'not-yet-inspected location' are not usable conditions; the replacement uses 'a different kind of location that does not appear in your recent observations'. I also aligned the carve-out with the examine-task desklamp and with u2. The preserved acquire-then-deliver behavior is supported by `P6:46`, `P6:48`, `P5:17`, `P5:18`. Whether 'recent observations' is an adequate proxy for 'already searched' is an unverified choice and could leave a still-closed container unchecked for a while; Memory is empty and provides no historical guidance.",
      "to": {
        "patch": "--- a/SKILL.md\n+++ b/SKILL.md\n@@\n-- During the search phase for a still-missing required object or tool, when you arrive at a container (drawer/cabinet/fridge) that is already open and was not opened by your immediately preceding action, treat it as already searched: do not re-examine its contents and do not re-open it.\n-- Instead, move to a closed container and open it, or to a location not observed earlier in the current search pass.\n-- Do not complete or repeat a cycle back through a set of containers already observed open or empty; if the most recent observed locations are containers you have already seen open, switch to a different, not-yet-inspected location.\n-- This rule applies only while the required object or tool is still missing; it does not apply once you are carrying the required object and are looking for the placement receptacle.\n+- During the search phase for a still-missing required object or tool, when the current observation shows that you have arrived at a container (drawer/cabinet/fridge) that is already open and that you did not open with your immediately preceding action, treat it as already searched: do not examine its contents and do not open it again.\n+- Read that as a signal that you are repeating a search you have already done: instead of stepping to the next container in the same rotation, switch to a different kind of location (for example a sidetable, dresser, coffeetable, armchair, sofa, bed, or garbagecan) that does not appear in your recent observations.\n+- Do not keep cycling through the same sequence of containers after you have returned to one that is already open; break the cycle by changing furniture type.\n+- This rule applies only while a required object or tool is still missing; it does not apply once you have acquired everything you need and are heading to the required next location (the desklamp for examine tasks, or the destination receptacle for pick-and-place).\n"
      }
    },
    {
      "target": "u2",
      "focus": ["S"],
      "change": "Localize the progression rule to signals the Actor has: after opening and checking the closed containers and inspecting the surfaces of the area being worked in while the required object or tool is still missing, move to a different furniture type or location that does not appear in the recent observations rather than returning to locations just visited, and each successive destination must be a different location or furniture type that is not just visited or observed. Keep the clause requiring immediate acquisition of a visible required object/tool and direct movement to the desklamp (examine tasks) or destination receptacle (pick-and-place).",
      "reason": "`G3` shows the parent repeatedly re-traversing the same subset: `P6` cycles drawers 1-6 and desk/shelf 1-2 from `P6:14` through `P6:44` and only finds the target after moving to sidetable 1 at `P6:45`; `P8` repeats drawers 1-3 (`P8:7`, `P8:11`) before finding the pencil on shelf 1 at `P8:12`; the efficient runs switch furniture type after one pass (`P10:11`). I keep this direction but replace the global 'not yet inspected in this pass' condition with a locally usable 'not in your recent observations' condition, because the Actor only has the current observation and the last two pre-action observation/action pairs. The preserved acquire-then-deliver clause is supported by `P6:46`, `P6:47`, `P6:48`, `P8:13`, `P8:15`, `P10:12`, `P10:14`. The local proxy is an unverified choice; Memory is empty and gives no historical guidance.",
      "to": {
        "patch": "--- a/SKILL.md\n+++ b/SKILL.md\n@@\n-- During the search phase, after checking the closed containers and surfaces currently reachable, if the required object or tool is still missing, proceed to a location or furniture type that has not yet been inspected in this pass (e.g., a sidetable, dresser, coffeetable, armchair, sofa, bed, or garbagecan) rather than re-traversing locations already checked.\n-- Each successive search destination should be a location that could plausibly hold the missing object/tool and has not already been observed in the current pass; do not re-enter the same limited subset of locations repeatedly.\n-- Once the required object or tool becomes visible, stop searching: take it immediately, and then go directly to the required next location (the desklamp for examine tasks, or the destination receptacle for pick-and-place) without further search moves.\n+- During the search phase, after you have opened and checked the closed containers and inspected the surfaces in the area you are working in and the required object or tool is still missing, move to a different furniture type or location that does not appear in your recent observations (for example a sidetable, dresser, coffeetable, armchair, sofa, bed, or garbagecan) rather than returning to locations you have just visited.\n+- Each successive search destination should be a different location or furniture type that could plausibly hold the missing object/tool and that you have not just visited or observed; do not re-enter the same limited subset of locations again and again.\n+- Once the required object or tool becomes visible, stop searching: take it immediately, and then go directly to the required next location (the desklamp for examine tasks, or the destination receptacle for pick-and-place) without further search moves.\n"
      }
    }
  ],
